### Vector Distance Mechanics & AVX-512 SIMD Execution

The core compute bottleneck of graph traversal is the distance calculation between the query vector $q$ and target node vectors $v$. For un-quantized vectors, Euclidean ($L_2$) distance and Inner Product ($IP$) are vectorized using AVX-512 Fused Multiply-Add (FMA) intrinsics [4].

For two vectors $\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v} \in \mathbb{R}^d$, squared Euclidean distance is defined as:

$$D_{L2}(\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}) = \sum_{i=0}^{d-1} (u_i - v_i)^2$$

In C++20 with AVX-512 intrinsics, the kernel unrolls across 512-bit registers (each holding 16 single-precision floats) to maximize pipeline throughput and instruction-level parallelism:

```cpp
#include <immintrin.h>
#include <cstddef>

// Fully vectorized AVX-512 squared Euclidean distance kernel
float avx512_l2_distance(const float* __restrict a, const float* __restrict b, size_t dim) {
    __m512 sum0 = _mm512_setzero_ps();
    __m512 sum1 = _mm512_setzero_ps();
    __m512 sum2 = _mm512_setzero_ps();
    __m512 sum3 = _mm512_setzero_ps();

    size_t i = 0;
    // Unroll loop processing 64 float32 elements per iteration (4 x 512-bit registers)
    for (; i + 63 < dim; i += 64) {
        __m512 va0 = _mm512_loadu_ps(a + i);
        __m512 vb0 = _mm512_loadu_ps(b + i);
        __m512 diff0 = _mm512_sub_ps(va0, vb0);
        sum0 = _mm512_fmadd_ps(diff0, diff0, sum0);

        __m512 va1 = _mm512_loadu_ps(a + i + 16);
        __m512 vb1 = _mm512_loadu_ps(b + i + 16);
        __m512 diff1 = _mm512_sub_ps(va1, vb1);
        sum1 = _mm512_fmadd_ps(diff1, diff1, sum1);

        __m512 va2 = _mm512_loadu_ps(a + i + 32);
        __m512 vb2 = _mm512_loadu_ps(b + i + 32);
        __m512 diff2 = _mm512_sub_ps(va2, vb2);
        sum2 = _mm512_fmadd_ps(diff2, diff2, sum2);

        __m512 va3 = _mm512_loadu_ps(a + i + 48);
        __m512 vb3 = _mm512_loadu_ps(b + i + 48);
        __m512 diff3 = _mm512_sub_ps(va3, vb3);
        sum3 = _mm512_fmadd_ps(diff3, diff3, sum3);
    }

    // Accumulate register groups
    sum0 = _mm512_add_ps(sum0, sum1);
    sum2 = _mm512_add_ps(sum2, sum3);
    sum0 = _mm512_add_ps(sum0, sum2);

    float acc = _mm512_reduce_add_ps(sum0);

    // Clean up remaining scalar elements
    for (; i < dim; ++i) {
        float diff = a[i] - b[i];
        acc += diff * diff;
    }

    return acc;
}
```

### Product Quantization (PQ) Compression Dynamics

To compress memory footprint by up to $16\times$ and minimize main memory fetch latency during candidate set expansion, vectors are compressed using Product Quantization (PQ) [2].

Vector space $\mathbb{R}^d$ is decomposed into $m$ orthogonal sub-spaces of dimension $d^* = d / m$. A codebook $C = \{c_1, c_2, \dots, c_k\}$ of $k=256$ centroids is computed per sub-space via $k$-means clustering, allowing each sub-vector to be represented by a single byte (`uint8_t`).

During query evaluation, Asymmetric Distance Computation (ADC) pre-computes distance lookup tables (LUT) between query sub-vectors and codebook centroids. Traversing a quantized node reduces distance estimation to $m$ byte lookups and scalar additions, dropping SIMD arithmetic bottlenecks into pure L1 cache lookup operations:

$$D_{ADC}(\mathbf{q}, \mathbf{v}) = \sum_{j=1}^{m} || q^{(j)} - c_j[v^{(j)}] ||^2$$

```
Query Sub-vector q^(j) ----> [ Pre-computed LUT (256 entries) ] ----> Distance Component
                                            ^ 
Codebook Centroid Index (uint8_t) ---------+
```

