### Lock-Free Concurrent HNSW Edge Replacement Architecture

Mutating neighbor sets concurrently without mutex locks requires copy-on-write semantics over lock-free atomic pointers combined with Epoch-Based Reclamation (EBR) [5]. The insertion algorithm executes the following sequence:

1. Locate nearest neighbors via multi-layer graph search.
2. Construct a new candidate `AdjacentLinks` structure with proposed updated edges.
3. Atomically swap pointer `layers[l]` via `Compare-And-Swap` (CAS).
4. Retire old `AdjacentLinks` object to the current EBR epoch collector.

```rust
pub fn lock_free_link_update(
    node: &GraphNode,
    layer: usize,
    new_neighbors: &[u32],
    epoch_guard: &EpochGuard,
) -> Result<(), CASFailure> {
    let new_links_ptr = Box::into_raw(Box::new(AdjacentLinks {
        count: AtomicU32::new(new_neighbors.len() as u32),
        capacity: 64,
        neighbors: {
            let mut arr = [0u32; 64];
            arr[..new_neighbors.len()].copy_from_slice(new_neighbors);
            arr
        },
    }));

    let layer_atomic = &node.layers[layer];
    let old_ptr = layer_atomic.load(Ordering::Acquire);

    // Execute atomic Compare-And-Swap
    match layer_atomic.compare_exchange(
        old_ptr,
        new_links_ptr,
        Ordering::Release,
        Ordering::Relaxed,
    ) {
        Ok(_) => {
            // Deferred garbage collection via EBR
            if !old_ptr.is_null() {
                epoch_guard.retire_ptr(old_ptr);
            }
            Ok(())
        }
        Err(_) => {
            // CAS failed: deallocate unused allocation and retry
            unsafe { drop(Box::from_raw(new_links_ptr)); }
            Err(CASFailure::Contention)
        }
    }
}
```

