             |                                      |                                    |
             |--- WriteState(Epoch 14, State X) --->|                                    |
             |                                      |-- [Epoch Check: 14 < 15]           |
             |                                      |   (Reject Write!)                  |
             |<-- Fencing Error (FENCE_OUTDATED) ---|                                    |
             |                                      |                                    |
    [Self-Terminate Actor]                          |                                    |
```

When Node A receives the `FENCE_OUTDATED` error, it immediately terminates its local actor instance and drops all buffered client connections, preventing split-brain state corruption.

### Adaptive Backpressure via BBR-inspired Rate Limiting

Agents make frequent, expensive calls to external LLM providers. During peak load, these providers return `HTTP 429 Too Many Requests` status codes. Standard exponential backoff algorithms fail in large-scale multi-tenant systems, as they lead to synchronization waves (the "thundering herd" problem).

We implement an **Adaptive Token Bucket** algorithm inspired by TCP BBR congestion control [5]. Instead of statically limiting requests, the Orchestration Plane monitors the round-trip latency ($\text{RTT}$) of LLM API calls and dynamically adjusts the maximum concurrency limit ($C_{\text{max}}$) per tenant using the following formulation:

$$C_{\text{max}} = \text{BBR}_{\text{target}} \times \frac{\text{RTT}_{\text{min}}}{\text{RTT}_{\text{current}}} \times \text{TokenBucket}_{\text{capacity}}$$

Where:
* $\text{RTT}_{\text{min}}$ is the minimum observed latency for a successful API call over a sliding window of 24 hours.
* $\text{RTT}_{\text{current}}$ is the exponentially smoothed moving average of recent API call latencies.
* $\text{BBR}_{\text{target}}$ is a tuning coefficient (typically $1.25$) allowing moderate exploration of the network's capacity frontier.

If $\text{RTT}_{\text{current}}$ spikes (indicating downstream queuing or imminent rate limits), $C_{\text{max}}$ is dynamically throttled. The actor mailbox senses this throttle and applies backpressure up the stack to the client by delaying gRPC stream acknowledgements (`ack_sequence_number`), causing the client’s TCP send buffer to fill and slowing down incoming traffic at the edge.

### Degraded Execution Paths

When external dependencies fail completely, the Orchestrator degrades gracefully using a tiered execution strategy:

| Tier | Downstream Health | Execution Strategy | Latency Profile | Accuracy / Fidelity |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Tier 1** | Green (Healthy) | Full Execution: Frontier LLM + Dynamic WASM Tools. | $1500\text{ms}$ - $5000\text{ms}$ | $100\%$ (Optimal) |
| **Tier 2** | Yellow (Degraded / Rate-Limited) | Fallback to highly optimized local edge models (e.g., Llama-3-8B) for non-critical reasoning steps. | $200\text{ms}$ - $800\text{ms}$ | $85\%$ (Reduced Reasoning) |
| **Tier 3** | Red (Outage / Partitioned) | Freeze execution loop. Write checkpoint state to local WAL disk buffer. Stream `AGENT_STATUS_IDLE` with structured pause metadata to client. | $<10\text{ms}

