## Section 5: Optimization Prescription & Production Implementation

To achieve minimal micro-architectural overhead under extreme concurrency, engineers must adhere to three core rules:
1. **Eliminate Dynamic Uprobes** on hot-path user functions; substitute them with kernel-level `fentry` probes attached to kernel system boundaries or eBPF USDT tracepoints.
2. **Avoid Shared Atomic Counters** across threads; deploy per-CPU data structures.
3. **Use Zero-Copy Ring Buffers** (`BPF_MAP_TYPE_RINGBUF`) instead of legacy perf event arrays (`BPF_MAP_TYPE_PERF_EVENT_ARRAY`) to eliminate duplicate kernel-to-user copy operations.

### Production eBPF Code: High-Throughput Kernel Tracing (`fentry` + Ring Buffer)

The following production C snippet demonstrates an optimized eBPF probe capturing socket write performance using `fentry` trampolines and zero-copy ring buffer allocations [4, 6].

```c
// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
#include "vmlinux.h"
#include <bpf/bpf_helpers.h>
#include <bpf/bpf_tracing.h>
#include <bpf/bpf_core_read.h>

char _license[] SEC("license") = "GPL";

// Definition of telemetry event structure aligned to 64-byte cache lines
struct event_t {
    u64 timestamp_ns;
    u64 duration_ns;
    u32 pid;
    u32 tgid;
    u64 bytes_written;
    char comm[16];
} __attribute__((packed, aligned(8)));

// Optimized Lock-Free Ring Buffer (Linux 5.8+)
struct {
    __uint(type, BPF_MAP_TYPE_RINGBUF);
    __uint(max_entries, 16 * 1024 * 1024); // 16 MB ring buffer
} ringbuf SEC(".maps");

// Map to store per-thread entry timestamps for latency tracking
struct {
    __uint(type, BPF_MAP_TYPE_HASH);
    __uint(max_entries, 10240);
    __type(key, u32);
    __type(value, u64);
} start_time SEC(".maps");

SEC("fentry/__x64_sys_write")
int BPF_PROG(trace_sys_write_entry, const struct pt_regs *regs) {
    u64 id = bpf_get_current_pid_tgid();
    u32 pid = (u32)id;
    u64 ts = bpf_ktime_get_ns();

    bpf_map_update_elem(&start_time, &pid, &ts, BPF_ANY);
    return 0;
}

SEC("fexit/__x64_sys_write")
int BPF_PROG(trace_sys_write_exit, const struct pt_regs *regs, long ret) {
    u64 id = bpf_get_current_pid_tgid();
    u32 pid = (u32)id;
    u64 *tsp = bpf_map_lookup_elem(&start_time, &pid);

    if (!tsp) {
        return 0; // Missed entry event
    }

    u64 duration = bpf_ktime_get_ns() - *tsp;
    bpf_map_delete_elem(&start_time, &pid);

    // Reserve space in Ring Buffer (Zero-Copy allocation)
    struct event_t *event = bpf_ringbuf_reserve(&ringbuf, sizeof(struct event_t), 0);
    if (!event) {
        return 0; // Ring buffer full: drop event cleanly
    }

    event->timestamp_ns = *tsp;
    event->duration_ns = duration;
    event->pid = pid;
    event->tgid = id >> 32;
    event->bytes_written = (ret > 0) ? ret : 0;
    bpf_get_current_comm(&event->comm, sizeof(event->comm));

    // Submit event to user-space lock-free ring reader
    bpf_ringbuf_submit(event, 0);
    return 0;
}
```

### High-Performance User-Space Ring Buffer Consumer (Rust)

The user-space ingestion runtime utilizes memory-mapped rings to drain metrics without incurring syscall lock contention.

```rust
use libbpf_rs::RingBufferBuilder;
use std::os::fd::AsRawFd;
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicBool, Ordering};
use std::sync::Arc;
use std::time::Duration;

#[repr(C, packed(8))]
#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone)]
pub struct Event {
    pub timestamp_ns: u64,
    pub duration_ns: u64,
    pub pid: u32,
    pub tgid: u32,
    pub bytes_written: u64,
    pub comm: [u8; 16],
}

pub struct TelemetryConsumer {
    running: Arc<AtomicBool>,
}

impl TelemetryConsumer {
    pub fn new(running: Arc<AtomicBool>) -> Self {
        Self { running }
    }

    pub fn consume_loop(&self, map_fd: i32) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
        let mut builder = RingBufferBuilder::new();

        // Callback directly receives memory-mapped reference from kernel buffer
        builder.add_fd(map_fd, move |data: &[u8]| {
            if data.len() < std::mem::size_of::<Event>() {
                return 0;
            }

            // Zero-copy cast from byte slice to Event struct reference
            let event = unsafe { &*(data.as_ptr() as *const Event) };

            // Process telemetry payload in-memory
            Self::process_event(event);
            0
        })?;

        let ringbuf = builder.build()?;

        // Polling loop with backoff mechanism
        while self.running.load(Ordering::Relaxed) {
            match ringbuf.poll(Duration::from_millis(10)) {
                Ok(_) => {},
                Err(e) => eprintln!("Error polling ring buffer: {:?}", e),
            }
        }

        Ok(())
    }

    #[inline(always)]
    fn process_event(event: &Event) {
        // High-speed thread-local aggregation or streaming engine offload
        core::hint::black_box(event);
    }
}
```

